public class ArmstrongNumbers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Armstrong numbers between 1 and 500:");
        for (int i = 1; i <= 500; i++) {
            int temp = i;
            int sum = 0;
            while (temp > 0) {
                int rem = temp % 10;
                sum += rem * rem * rem;
                temp /= 10;
            }
            if (sum == i) {
                System.out.print(i + " ");
            }
        }
    }
}
Explanation: While technically using a while loop nested inside a for loop (a common pattern for processing individual numbers in a range), the outer for loop iterates through every candidate number from 1 to 500. The inner loop/process extracts each digit using the modulo operator (temp % 10), cubes it, adds it to 'sum', and reduces 'temp' using integer division (temp / 10). If the sum of cubes equals the original number 'i', it is printed as an Armstrong number.

