Program Solution:
public class PerfectNumber {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Perfect numbers between 1 and 1000:");
        for (int i = 1; i <= 1000; i++) {
            int sum = 0;
            for (int j = 1; j < i; j++) {
                if (i % j == 0) {
                    sum += j;
                }
            }
            if (sum == i) {
                System.out.print(i + " ");
            }
        }
    }
}
Explanation: The outer loop tests each number 'i' from 1 to 1000. For each candidate 'i', the inner loop iterates through all integers 'j' from 1 up to i-1 to check if 'j' is a proper divisor of 'i' (i.e., i % j == 0). If true, 'j' is added to 'sum'. After the inner loop completes, if 'sum' equals 'i', the number is classified and printed as a perfect number (e.g., 6, 28, 496).

Program Solution:
public class StarPyramid {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            // Print leading spaces
            for (int j = 1; j <= n - i; j++) {
                System.out.print(" ");
            }
            // Print stars
            for (int k = 1; k <= (2 * i - 1); k++) {
                System.out.print("*");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
Explanation: The outer loop controls the row progression from 1 to 5. For each row 'i', two distinct inner loops are utilized: the first inner loop prints the required leading spaces (n - i spaces) to center-align the pyramid, and the second inner loop prints the stars (2 * i - 1 stars per row). After both inner loops execute for a given row, `System.out.println()` shifts output to the next line.

