        System.out.println("Sum (2): " + seriesSum(3.0));
        System.out.println("Sum (3): " + seriesSum(4L));
    }
}

1. Static Methods: Belong to the class rather than any object. They can be called without creating an instance of the class using the class name. They cannot access non-static instance variables or non-static methods directly.
2. Non-static Methods: Belong to an object of the class and require object instantiation to be invoked. They can access both static and non-static members.

Reason: Static methods are loaded into memory when the class loads, before any objects are instantiated. Hence, non-static variables do not exist yet.

Example code:
class Demo {
    int x = 10; // instance variable
    static int y = 20; // static variable

    static void display() {
        // System.out.println(x); // ERROR: Cannot make a static reference to the non-static field x
        System.out.println("Static y: " + y);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Demo.display();
    }
}

